ELEMENTARY ANALYTICAL METHODS (9.10):
3.10. Theorems on Continued Fractions Definitions
3.10.1   f=b_{0}+\frac{a_{1}}{b_{1}+a_{2}}\frac{a_{3}}{b_{3}+\frac{a_{4}}{b_{5}+\cdots}}\]
If the number of terms is finite, f is called a terminating continued fraction. If the number of terms is infinite, f is called an infinite continued fraction and the terminating fraction
is called the nth convergent of f.
2. If \lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{A_{n}}{B_{n}} exists, the infinite continued fraction f is said to be convergent. If a_{i}=1 and the b_{i} are integers there is always convergence.
3.10.1   f=b_{0}+\frac{a_{1}}{b_{1}+a_{2}}\frac{a_{3}}{b_{3}+\cdots}\frac{a_{n}}{b_{n}}\]
If the number of terms is finite, f is called a terminating continued fraction. If the number of terms is infinite, f is called an infinite continued fraction and the terminating fraction
is called the nth convergent of f.
3.2   If \lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{A_{n}}{B_{n}} exists, the infinite continued fraction f is said to be convergent. If a_{i}=1 and the b_{i} are integers there is always convergence.
3.3.10.2   f=b_{0}+\frac{a_{1}}{b_{1}+a_{2}}\frac{a_{3}}{b_{3}+\cdots}\frac{a_{n}}{b_{n}}\]
If the number of terms is finite, f is called a terminating continued fraction. If the number of terms is infinite, f is called an infinite continued fraction and the terminating fraction
is called the nth convergent of f.
